139
00:06:23,470 --> 00:06:26,110
Next, I'll give a really
brief intro about transformers

140
00:06:26,110 --> 00:06:29,020
and how attention works.

141
00:06:29,020 --> 00:06:33,200
So the first step for
language is word embeddings.

142
00:06:33,200 --> 00:06:36,220
So words aren't numbers,
so we obviously can't just

143
00:06:36,220 --> 00:06:38,000
pass them into a model as is.

144
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:39,790
So the first step
is converting them

145
00:06:39,790 --> 00:06:42,610
into dense vectors in a
high-dimensional space.

146
00:06:42,610 --> 00:06:44,330
This is done through
various methods,

147
00:06:44,330 --> 00:06:47,650
but the goal is to capture
semantic similarity.

148
00:06:47,650 --> 00:06:49,330
Essentially, that
cat and dog are

149
00:06:49,330 --> 00:06:51,110
more similar than
cat and car even

150
00:06:51,110 --> 00:06:53,510
though the latter is more
similar from a character

151
00:06:53,510 --> 00:06:54,770
standpoint.

152
00:06:54,770 --> 00:06:57,650
Doing so enables
visualization, learning

153
00:06:57,650 --> 00:06:58,940
with transformer models.

154
00:06:58,940 --> 00:07:01,580
Or arithmetic like
I've shown like king

155
00:07:01,580 --> 00:07:03,950
minus man plus queen
would approximately be

156
00:07:03,950 --> 00:07:05,850
queen in some embedding space.

157
00:07:05,850 --> 00:07:10,340
And classical methods for this
are like Word2Vec, FastTex,

158
00:07:10,340 --> 00:07:11,910
and many more these days.

159
00:07:11,910 --> 00:07:17,730
But static embeddings, for
instance, giving the word bank.

160
00:07:17,730 --> 00:07:21,020
The same meaning and
just bank as in riverbank

161
00:07:21,020 --> 00:07:22,470
have limitations.

162
00:07:22,470 --> 00:07:25,860
Therefore, the current standard
is using contextual embeddings,

163
00:07:25,860 --> 00:07:29,240
which take into account the
context and the sentence

164
00:07:29,240 --> 00:07:32,120
that a word is in.

165
00:07:32,120 --> 00:07:34,790
Self-attention can
be applied to this

166
00:07:34,790 --> 00:07:37,530
to learn what to focus
on for a given token.

167
00:07:37,530 --> 00:07:42,620
So to do this, you learn
three matrices, a query, key,

168
00:07:42,620 --> 00:07:46,670
and value, which together
comprise the attention process.

169
00:07:46,670 --> 00:07:48,500
A quick analogy
for this is imagine

170
00:07:48,500 --> 00:07:51,880
you're in a library looking
for a book on a certain topic.

171
00:07:51,880 --> 00:07:53,880
This would be your query.

172
00:07:53,880 --> 00:07:57,810
Now, let's say each book
has some summary associated

173
00:07:57,810 --> 00:07:59,670
with it, a key.

174
00:07:59,670 --> 00:08:03,870
You can match your query and
key and get access to the book

175
00:08:03,870 --> 00:08:05,020
that you're looking for.

176
00:08:05,020 --> 00:08:08,010
The information inside the
book would be your value.

177
00:08:08,010 --> 00:08:12,900
So in attention, we do a
soft match over the values

178
00:08:12,900 --> 00:08:15,340
to get info from,
say, multiple books,

179
00:08:15,340 --> 00:08:19,860
and this comprises the
attention operation.

180
00:08:19,860 --> 00:08:22,812
And as you can see in
this visualization, when

181
00:08:22,812 --> 00:08:24,270
you apply this to
language, you can

182
00:08:24,270 --> 00:08:27,070
see that across different
layers of the model,

183
00:08:27,070 --> 00:08:30,810
different words have
connections to the rest

184
00:08:30,810 --> 00:08:34,140
of the words in the sentence.

185
00:08:34,140 --> 00:08:38,580
The next component is positional
encodings or embeddings, which

186
00:08:38,580 --> 00:08:40,240
add order to the sequence.

187
00:08:40,240 --> 00:08:42,659
Without these, the
model, since you

188
00:08:42,659 --> 00:08:45,760
have just linear
multiplications here,

189
00:08:45,760 --> 00:08:48,740
would not know what the first
or the last word in the sentence

190
00:08:48,740 --> 00:08:49,240
is.

191
00:08:49,240 --> 00:08:52,110
Therefore, you add
some notion of order

192
00:08:52,110 --> 00:08:53,753
through, say, sinusoids.

193
00:08:53,753 --> 00:08:55,170
Or in the simplest
form, you could

194
00:08:55,170 --> 00:08:57,785
think that the first word
would be zero, the second one,

195
00:08:57,785 --> 00:09:00,690
the one, and on.

196
00:09:00,690 --> 00:09:04,230
Beyond this is basically just
scaling through multiple layers

197
00:09:04,230 --> 00:09:06,690
and multi-head
attention, more heads

198
00:09:06,690 --> 00:09:08,650
to attend to different
parts of the sentence.

199
00:09:08,650 --> 00:09:10,110
And more parameters
means that you

200
00:09:10,110 --> 00:09:12,210
can capture more
diverse relationships

201
00:09:12,210 --> 00:09:13,980
from your sequences.

202
00:09:13,980 --> 00:09:16,870
And this gives you
the final transformer.

203
00:09:16,870 --> 00:09:20,400

204
00:09:20,400 --> 00:09:22,920
Transformers today
have overtaken

205
00:09:22,920 --> 00:09:27,570
pretty much every field from
LLMs like GPT-4o, o3, DeepSeek,

206
00:09:27,570 --> 00:09:30,360
to vision with models that are
getting increasingly better

207
00:09:30,360 --> 00:09:32,670
at segmentation and whatnot.

208
00:09:32,670 --> 00:09:37,770
Speech, biology, video, you'll
see a lot of these applications

209
00:09:37,770 --> 00:09:39,240
throughout the quarter.

210
00:09:39,240 --> 00:09:41,790
With large language models,
these are essentially

211
00:09:41,790 --> 00:09:45,250
just scaled up versions of
attention and the transformer

212
00:09:45,250 --> 00:09:46,720
architecture.

213
00:09:46,720 --> 00:09:49,730
You essentially just throw
a large amount of data,

214
00:09:49,730 --> 00:09:53,030
general text data derived
from the web, at these models

215
00:09:53,030 --> 00:09:55,060
and they can learn
very well to model

216
00:09:55,060 --> 00:09:58,870
through a next token
prediction objective language.

217
00:09:58,870 --> 00:10:02,750
And as you scale up, we've seen
that emergent abilities pop up.

218
00:10:02,750 --> 00:10:04,660
So while at a smaller
scale, you might not

219
00:10:04,660 --> 00:10:06,582
be able to do a certain task.

220
00:10:06,582 --> 00:10:08,290
Once you get to a
certain scale, you just

221
00:10:08,290 --> 00:10:12,140
have a peak in the
ability to do that task.

222
00:10:12,140 --> 00:10:14,620
Some disadvantages,
though, that these models

223
00:10:14,620 --> 00:10:17,620
have very high computational
costs and therefore also

224
00:10:17,620 --> 00:10:21,070
concerns with climate
and the carbon emissions

225
00:10:21,070 --> 00:10:23,230
they may produce.

226
00:10:23,230 --> 00:10:27,670
And like I was mentioning
with larger models,

227
00:10:27,670 --> 00:10:31,690
they're very good at
generalizing to many abilities

228
00:10:31,690 --> 00:10:33,490
or tasks, and
they're essentially

229
00:10:33,490 --> 00:10:35,505
plug-and-play with few
or zero-shot learning.

230
00:10:35,505 --> 00:10:40,270

231
00:10:40,270 --> 00:10:40,820
All right.

232
00:10:40,820 --> 00:10:43,160
So now I'll talk a bit
more about pretraining.

233
00:10:43,160 --> 00:10:46,320
So as Karan explained how
the transformer works.

234
00:10:46,320 --> 00:10:48,860
But typically with a
language model, especially

235
00:10:48,860 --> 00:10:50,780
a large language
model, you typically

236
00:10:50,780 --> 00:10:53,390
divide it into two stages,
pretraining stage where

237
00:10:53,390 --> 00:10:55,350
you train the neural
network from scratch

238
00:10:55,350 --> 00:10:58,190
from randomized or
initialized weights, randomly

239
00:10:58,190 --> 00:11:01,700
initialized weights to give
it more general capabilities.

240
00:11:01,700 --> 00:11:05,160
And a big portion of
this is the data itself.

241
00:11:05,160 --> 00:11:07,850
So the data is the
fundamental fuel

242
00:11:07,850 --> 00:11:10,940
that allows your model to
learn, because that's what

243
00:11:10,940 --> 00:11:13,340
the model is learning from.

244
00:11:13,340 --> 00:11:16,190
So your goal typically, again,
like I said with pretraining,

245
00:11:16,190 --> 00:11:18,170
is to train on a
large amount of data

246
00:11:18,170 --> 00:11:21,650
to obtain some sort of
general level of capabilities

247
00:11:21,650 --> 00:11:24,380
and overall knowledge
or intelligence.

248
00:11:24,380 --> 00:11:26,990
And this is arguably, again,
the most important aspect

249
00:11:26,990 --> 00:11:29,870
of training,
especially pretraining.

250
00:11:29,870 --> 00:11:32,090
Especially because
LLMs learn, again,

251
00:11:32,090 --> 00:11:34,190
based on statistical
distributions predicting

252
00:11:34,190 --> 00:11:37,340
the next token given
previous tokens.

253
00:11:37,340 --> 00:11:39,740
So to effectively learn
this, you typically

254
00:11:39,740 --> 00:11:42,050
need a large amount of data.

255
00:11:42,050 --> 00:11:45,370
So because of its importance,
how do we maximally leverage it?

256
00:11:45,370 --> 00:11:50,640
So again, smart data
strategies for pretraining

257
00:11:50,640 --> 00:11:54,600
is definitely one of the most
important topics these days.

258
00:11:54,600 --> 00:11:56,430
So I'll briefly touch
upon two of the topic

259
00:11:56,430 --> 00:11:59,830
projects I recently worked
on two different scales.

260
00:11:59,830 --> 00:12:02,490
The first is looking
at what makes small,

261
00:12:02,490 --> 00:12:05,190
child-like data sets potentially
effective for language

262
00:12:05,190 --> 00:12:07,600
learning especially
on the smaller scale.

263
00:12:07,600 --> 00:12:11,310
And the second is looking
at smart data strategies

264
00:12:11,310 --> 00:12:16,540
for training large models on
billions or trillions of tokens,

265
00:12:16,540 --> 00:12:19,560
which is on a much larger scale.

266
00:12:19,560 --> 00:12:24,060
So why are humans able
to learn so efficiently?

267
00:12:24,060 --> 00:12:27,985
This looks at how human
children learn and interact

268
00:12:27,985 --> 00:12:29,610
with the environment
and learn language

269
00:12:29,610 --> 00:12:31,800
compared to a model
like ChatGPT, which

270
00:12:31,800 --> 00:12:34,553
is a bit analogous to how
the human brain learns

271
00:12:34,553 --> 00:12:36,720
language and learns in
general compared to something

272
00:12:36,720 --> 00:12:38,820
like a neural network.

273
00:12:38,820 --> 00:12:40,500
So some potential
key differences

274
00:12:40,500 --> 00:12:42,790
are that humans
learn continuously.

275
00:12:42,790 --> 00:12:44,270
We're continually learning.

276
00:12:44,270 --> 00:12:45,513
We don't just pretrain.

277
00:12:45,513 --> 00:12:46,930
We don't just sit
in a chair, have

278
00:12:46,930 --> 00:12:48,513
someone read the
whole internet to us,

279
00:12:48,513 --> 00:12:51,220
and then we just stop
learning from there.

280
00:12:51,220 --> 00:12:54,340
So that's unlike a lot of
current models, which are more

281
00:12:54,340 --> 00:12:56,800
single pass pretraining models.

282
00:12:56,800 --> 00:13:00,100
Further, we have more
goal-based approaches

283
00:13:00,100 --> 00:13:02,240
to learning and interaction
with the environment.

284
00:13:02,240 --> 00:13:03,910
That's a major reason we learn.

285
00:13:03,910 --> 00:13:06,400
Whereas, again, these models
are typically just pretraining

286
00:13:06,400 --> 00:13:08,770
on large amounts of data
using next token prediction

287
00:13:08,770 --> 00:13:10,300
or autoregression.

288
00:13:10,300 --> 00:13:12,700
Further, we learned through
continuous multimodal

289
00:13:12,700 --> 00:13:13,970
or multisensory data.

290
00:13:13,970 --> 00:13:15,560
So it's not just text only.

291
00:13:15,560 --> 00:13:18,010
We're subconsciously
exposed to probably hundreds

292
00:13:18,010 --> 00:13:22,030
of sensors that guide the
way we learn and approach

293
00:13:22,030 --> 00:13:23,740
our daily lives.

294
00:13:23,740 --> 00:13:25,960
Further, I believe our
brains are fundamentally

295
00:13:25,960 --> 00:13:27,820
different in that
we learn probably

296
00:13:27,820 --> 00:13:30,260
in more structured or
hierarchical manners,

297
00:13:30,260 --> 00:13:32,380
for example, through
compositionality rather

298
00:13:32,380 --> 00:13:35,020
than, again, simply
next token prediction.

299
00:13:35,020 --> 00:13:36,940
And the focus of this
project in particular

300
00:13:36,940 --> 00:13:38,780
is more on the data differences.

301
00:13:38,780 --> 00:13:43,600
So again, humans are exposed to
dialogue from people we talk to,

302
00:13:43,600 --> 00:13:46,630
storybooks,
especially children--

303
00:13:46,630 --> 00:13:52,300
compared to large amounts
of data on the internet.

304
00:13:52,300 --> 00:13:55,345
So this is a work that
was published-- so why?

305
00:13:55,345 --> 00:13:58,060
Why do you care about
small models and training

306
00:13:58,060 --> 00:13:59,353
on small amounts of data?

307
00:13:59,353 --> 00:14:01,270
Well, this will greatly
improve the efficiency

308
00:14:01,270 --> 00:14:03,590
of training and using
large language models,

309
00:14:03,590 --> 00:14:06,170
and this will open the door
to potential new use cases,

310
00:14:06,170 --> 00:14:09,298
for example, models that
can run on your phone

311
00:14:09,298 --> 00:14:10,840
that you can run
locally and so forth

312
00:14:10,840 --> 00:14:12,700
for many different use cases.

313
00:14:12,700 --> 00:14:14,980
Smaller models and
train on less data

314
00:14:14,980 --> 00:14:18,730
are also more interpretable
and easier to control or align

315
00:14:18,730 --> 00:14:21,190
whether it's for safety
purposes to reduce bias

316
00:14:21,190 --> 00:14:25,990
and so forth to ensure people
are using them for safe reasons

317
00:14:25,990 --> 00:14:28,150
and you have appropriate
guardrails in place.

318
00:14:28,150 --> 00:14:31,330
This will also enhance the open
source availability allowing

319
00:14:31,330 --> 00:14:33,700
research and the usage
of these models for more

320
00:14:33,700 --> 00:14:36,100
people around the world
rather than simply companies

321
00:14:36,100 --> 00:14:37,990
with large amounts of compute.

322
00:14:37,990 --> 00:14:40,640
And in general, this might
even allow us to more

323
00:14:40,640 --> 00:14:42,830
greatly understand the
other direction, which

324
00:14:42,830 --> 00:14:46,410
is how humans are able to learn
so effectively and efficiently.

325
00:14:46,410 --> 00:14:49,250

326
00:14:49,250 --> 00:14:51,830
Yep, so this work is titled
Is Child-Directed Speech.

327
00:14:51,830 --> 00:14:53,990
Effective Training Data
for Language Models,

328
00:14:53,990 --> 00:14:59,120
which I presented at EMNLP
in Miami last November.

329
00:14:59,120 --> 00:15:02,210
So again, the hypothesis
here is that children,

330
00:15:02,210 --> 00:15:04,880
we probably learned
fundamentally different

331
00:15:04,880 --> 00:15:05,792
from LLMs.

332
00:15:05,792 --> 00:15:08,000
This is why we're able to
learn on several magnitudes

333
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:10,500
less language data, in
particular than many

334
00:15:10,500 --> 00:15:12,500
of these large language
models these days, which

335
00:15:12,500 --> 00:15:14,130
require trillions of tokens.

336
00:15:14,130 --> 00:15:15,960
Now, there's several hypotheses.

337
00:15:15,960 --> 00:15:19,880
One is the data we receive
as humans is different

338
00:15:19,880 --> 00:15:21,650
fundamentally from LLMs.

339
00:15:21,650 --> 00:15:23,660
Rather than just training
on internet data,

340
00:15:23,660 --> 00:15:25,230
we actually interact
with people.

341
00:15:25,230 --> 00:15:26,570
We talk to people.

342
00:15:26,570 --> 00:15:31,380
We hear stories that our
parents or teachers tell us

343
00:15:31,380 --> 00:15:32,100
and so forth.

344
00:15:32,100 --> 00:15:34,768
The other is maybe
the human brain just

345
00:15:34,768 --> 00:15:36,060
fundamentally learns different.

346
00:15:36,060 --> 00:15:37,435
So our learning
algorithm is just

347
00:15:37,435 --> 00:15:39,460
different from large
language models.

348
00:15:39,460 --> 00:15:43,230
And another is maybe it's the
way or the structure in which we

349
00:15:43,230 --> 00:15:44,080
receive this data.

350
00:15:44,080 --> 00:15:47,850
So any data we receive is
somewhat curricularized.

351
00:15:47,850 --> 00:15:51,300
We start off with simple data,
simple language as a child

352
00:15:51,300 --> 00:15:53,800
and then learn more
complex grammars.

353
00:15:53,800 --> 00:15:57,300
We hear more complex
speech from our parents,

354
00:15:57,300 --> 00:15:59,010
co-workers, and so forth.

355
00:15:59,010 --> 00:16:00,840
Anything we do whether
it's learning math,

356
00:16:00,840 --> 00:16:04,660
we start simple and then move
on to more difficult problems.

357
00:16:04,660 --> 00:16:06,270
Whereas language
models, you typically

358
00:16:06,270 --> 00:16:08,910
don't care too much about
ordering or curriculum,

359
00:16:08,910 --> 00:16:11,550
so there's multiple
different hypotheses here.

360
00:16:11,550 --> 00:16:13,530
So in order to test some
of these, what we did

361
00:16:13,530 --> 00:16:16,020
is we trained some small
GPT-2 and RoBERTa models

362
00:16:16,020 --> 00:16:17,940
on five different data sets.

363
00:16:17,940 --> 00:16:21,720
One is CHILDES which is
a natural conversation

364
00:16:21,720 --> 00:16:22,600
data with children.

365
00:16:22,600 --> 00:16:24,270
So this is transcribed.

366
00:16:24,270 --> 00:16:26,010
And then we collected
a synthetic version

367
00:16:26,010 --> 00:16:28,620
called TinyDialogues, which
I'll discuss more later.

368
00:16:28,620 --> 00:16:30,900
BabyLM, which is
a diverse mixture

369
00:16:30,900 --> 00:16:32,230
of different types of data.

370
00:16:32,230 --> 00:16:34,780
This includes Reddit data,
Wikipedia data, and so forth.

371
00:16:34,780 --> 00:16:37,980
So this is closer to your
typical large language model

372
00:16:37,980 --> 00:16:38,808
pretraining data.

373
00:16:38,808 --> 00:16:40,350
And then we also
did a bit of testing

374
00:16:40,350 --> 00:16:43,210
with Wikipedia as
well OpenSubtitles,

375
00:16:43,210 --> 00:16:45,220
so movie and TV transcriptions.

376
00:16:45,220 --> 00:16:49,140
So we collected
TinyDialogues, and this

377
00:16:49,140 --> 00:16:52,050
was inspired by the fact
that a lot of, again, I said,

378
00:16:52,050 --> 00:16:55,110
our learning as children
is through conversations

379
00:16:55,110 --> 00:16:56,250
with other people.

380
00:16:56,250 --> 00:16:58,740
And conversations
naturally lead to learning.

381
00:16:58,740 --> 00:16:59,903
We talk to someone.

382
00:16:59,903 --> 00:17:00,820
They give us feedback.

383
00:17:00,820 --> 00:17:02,530
We reflect on how the
conversation went.

384
00:17:02,530 --> 00:17:05,670
So it's both pure
and self-reflection.

385
00:17:05,670 --> 00:17:08,490
Furthermore, conversations
lead to not only learning

386
00:17:08,490 --> 00:17:10,750
of knowledge but other things
like ethics and morals,

387
00:17:10,750 --> 00:17:13,349
for example, parents or
teachers telling us as children

388
00:17:13,349 --> 00:17:15,970
what's right or wrong to do.

389
00:17:15,970 --> 00:17:18,089
And there's many different
types of conversations

390
00:17:18,089 --> 00:17:20,190
you can have with many
different types of people

391
00:17:20,190 --> 00:17:22,390
leading to a lot of
diversity and learning.

392
00:17:22,390 --> 00:17:24,510
So what we did is
we collected a fully

393
00:17:24,510 --> 00:17:27,180
grammatical and
curricularized conversation

394
00:17:27,180 --> 00:17:29,610
data set with a limited
child-like restricted

395
00:17:29,610 --> 00:17:33,540
vocabulary using GPT-4, and we
collected different examples

396
00:17:33,540 --> 00:17:37,460
that differ by child age,
the different participants

397
00:17:37,460 --> 00:17:40,580
in the conversation,
and so forth.

398
00:17:40,580 --> 00:17:45,350
And here's just some
examples of some data points

399
00:17:45,350 --> 00:17:46,530
in our collected data set.

400
00:17:46,530 --> 00:17:50,660
So you see, as the age goes up,
the utterances or conversations

401
00:17:50,660 --> 00:17:51,630
become more complex.

402
00:17:51,630 --> 00:17:52,590
They become longer.

403
00:17:52,590 --> 00:17:59,240
The participants also
differ by age appropriately.

404
00:17:59,240 --> 00:18:02,300
So we also ran an experiment,
a curriculum experiment

405
00:18:02,300 --> 00:18:06,380
where we ordered either
by ascending age order.

406
00:18:06,380 --> 00:18:09,875
So the model will first see
two-year-old conversations

407
00:18:09,875 --> 00:18:11,750
and then five-year-old
conversations and then

408
00:18:11,750 --> 00:18:15,330
10-year-old, and so forth,
versus descending order.

409
00:18:15,330 --> 00:18:17,600
Maybe it's possible a
language model might actually

410
00:18:17,600 --> 00:18:20,790
learn somehow better from
more complex examples first

411
00:18:20,790 --> 00:18:23,690
and then of course the typical
baseline of randomly shuffling

412
00:18:23,690 --> 00:18:25,110
all your data examples.

413
00:18:25,110 --> 00:18:28,970
So we have some basic
evaluation metrics targeted

414
00:18:28,970 --> 00:18:30,510
at fundamental capabilities.

415
00:18:30,510 --> 00:18:33,450
One is basic grammatical
and syntactic knowledge,

416
00:18:33,450 --> 00:18:36,350
and another is a
free word association

417
00:18:36,350 --> 00:18:39,380
metric called Word
Similarity for assessing

418
00:18:39,380 --> 00:18:40,967
more semantic knowledge.

419
00:18:40,967 --> 00:18:42,800
So you see here from
the different data sets

420
00:18:42,800 --> 00:18:45,092
that actually it seems like
training on child-like data

421
00:18:45,092 --> 00:18:47,540
is worse than a
heterogeneous mixture of just

422
00:18:47,540 --> 00:18:49,280
internet data like BabyLM.

423
00:18:49,280 --> 00:18:52,250
So both metrics degrade quite
substantially especially

424
00:18:52,250 --> 00:18:56,750
on CHILDES, the more
natural conversation

425
00:18:56,750 --> 00:18:59,880
data set between children
and their caregivers.

426
00:18:59,880 --> 00:19:02,900
And you'll see, in
terms of curriculum,

427
00:19:02,900 --> 00:19:05,570
we don't see many substantial
differences no matter

428
00:19:05,570 --> 00:19:08,780
what order you provide the
examples into the model, which

429
00:19:08,780 --> 00:19:11,750
is, again, surprising,
because, as humans, we go

430
00:19:11,750 --> 00:19:13,880
from simple to more difficult.

431
00:19:13,880 --> 00:19:17,120
So looking more closely at
convergence behavior or loss

432
00:19:17,120 --> 00:19:20,240
curves, you'll see
here that the training

433
00:19:20,240 --> 00:19:28,550
loss, has these sorts of--

434
00:19:28,550 --> 00:19:32,140
a cyclical, depending on the
buckets you use for curriculum.

435
00:19:32,140 --> 00:19:34,230
But the validation loss,
which is what you really

436
00:19:34,230 --> 00:19:36,870
care about so the
generalization and learning, it

437
00:19:36,870 --> 00:19:38,310
has the exact same
trend no matter

438
00:19:38,310 --> 00:19:41,950
what order you feed the
examples in, which is, again,

439
00:19:41,950 --> 00:19:43,870
a very interesting finding.

440
00:19:43,870 --> 00:19:46,685
So overall, we see that diverse
data sources like BabyLM

441
00:19:46,685 --> 00:19:48,060
seem to provide
a better learning

442
00:19:48,060 --> 00:19:51,940
signal for language models than
purely child-directed speech.

443
00:19:51,940 --> 00:19:54,180
And we do see, however,
that our TinyDialogues data

444
00:19:54,180 --> 00:19:57,070
set noticeably outperforms the
natural conversation data set,

445
00:19:57,070 --> 00:20:00,180
likely because that data set
is very noisy whereas ours

446
00:20:00,180 --> 00:20:03,160
is, again, synthetically
collected by GPT-4.

447
00:20:03,160 --> 00:20:05,635
And again, global
developmental ordering

448
00:20:05,635 --> 00:20:08,010
using curriculum learning
seems to have negligible impact

449
00:20:08,010 --> 00:20:09,220
on performance.

450
00:20:09,220 --> 00:20:13,050
So overall, we can
conclude that it's possible

451
00:20:13,050 --> 00:20:15,460
that other aspects of
children's learning,

452
00:20:15,460 --> 00:20:17,160
not simply the data
they're exposed to,

453
00:20:17,160 --> 00:20:19,660
are responsible for their
efficient language learning,

454
00:20:19,660 --> 00:20:22,200
for example, learning from
other types of information

455
00:20:22,200 --> 00:20:25,500
like multimodal information, or
it's the fact that our learning

456
00:20:25,500 --> 00:20:28,200
algorithm in our brain is
just fundamentally different

457
00:20:28,200 --> 00:20:32,260
and more data efficient than
language modeling techniques.

458
00:20:32,260 --> 00:20:37,190
So if you wish to learn more, we
have our data set released on.

459
00:20:37,190 --> 00:20:39,580
Hugging Face as
well as on GitHub.

460
00:20:39,580 --> 00:20:43,840
And the papers up
on arXiv as well.

461
00:20:43,840 --> 00:20:45,770
So now, let's go bigger scale.

462
00:20:45,770 --> 00:20:48,220
So we were investigating
small models

463
00:20:48,220 --> 00:20:50,990
trained on small amounts of
data similar to a human child.

464
00:20:50,990 --> 00:20:53,770
Now what about current large
models, billions of parameters

465
00:20:53,770 --> 00:20:55,720
trained on trillions of tokens?

466
00:20:55,720 --> 00:20:58,370
So during my last
summer internship,

467
00:20:58,370 --> 00:21:01,150
I worked on a project with
NVIDIA titled Maximize Your

468
00:21:01,150 --> 00:21:03,850
Data's Potential,
Enhancing LLM Accuracy

469
00:21:03,850 --> 00:21:05,750
with Two-Phase Pretraining.

470
00:21:05,750 --> 00:21:09,910
So this is the optimized
data selection as well as

471
00:21:09,910 --> 00:21:13,060
training strategies in
large scale pre-training.

472
00:21:13,060 --> 00:21:15,490
So a lot of works like Llama
highlighted the effectiveness

473
00:21:15,490 --> 00:21:18,220
of different sorts
of data mixtures,

474
00:21:18,220 --> 00:21:21,490
but they don't really shed
light into the exact mixtures

475
00:21:21,490 --> 00:21:23,650
and how these
decisions were made.

476
00:21:23,650 --> 00:21:26,320
Whereas, we know data
blending and ordering

477
00:21:26,320 --> 00:21:28,820
is crucial to effective
LLM pretraining.

478
00:21:28,820 --> 00:21:30,680
So can we shed
more light on this,

479
00:21:30,680 --> 00:21:32,180
which is what our work does?

480
00:21:32,180 --> 00:21:34,540
So firstly, we formalize
and systematically

481
00:21:34,540 --> 00:21:38,080
evaluate this concept of
two-phase pre-training.

482
00:21:38,080 --> 00:21:39,580
And we show that,
empirically, it

483
00:21:39,580 --> 00:21:42,258
improves over continuous
training, which is typically

484
00:21:42,258 --> 00:21:44,050
what's done with LLM
training, and you just

485
00:21:44,050 --> 00:21:46,180
feed in all the data
rather than separating it

486
00:21:46,180 --> 00:21:50,210
into particular buckets
or a different schedule.

487
00:21:50,210 --> 00:21:51,850
We also do a
fine-grained analysis

488
00:21:51,850 --> 00:21:56,260
of data blending for these
two pretraining phases.

489
00:21:56,260 --> 00:21:59,890
And we have this notion of
prototyping blends on smaller

490
00:21:59,890 --> 00:22:01,730
token counts before scaling up.

491
00:22:01,730 --> 00:22:03,820
So this two-phase
pretraining approach

492
00:22:03,820 --> 00:22:07,840
is inspired kind of by how
pretraining and post-training

493
00:22:07,840 --> 00:22:11,530
works, which is the first
phase is on more general data.

494
00:22:11,530 --> 00:22:13,780
So this is to
learn more broadly.

495
00:22:13,780 --> 00:22:15,290
So it's on more diverse data.

496
00:22:15,290 --> 00:22:17,260
And the second is
to shift to more

497
00:22:17,260 --> 00:22:20,660
high-quality, domain-specific
data such as math and so forth.

498
00:22:20,660 --> 00:22:23,290
However, it's important
to balance between quality

499
00:22:23,290 --> 00:22:25,480
and diversity in both
phases, as if you

500
00:22:25,480 --> 00:22:30,770
operate any data set too much,
it can lead to overfitting.

501
00:22:30,770 --> 00:22:34,290
So firstly, does two-phase
training actually help?

502
00:22:34,290 --> 00:22:37,370
So we found that all
our Phase 2 blends

503
00:22:37,370 --> 00:22:40,400
or our two-phase
pretraining experiments

504
00:22:40,400 --> 00:22:43,910
outperformed the baseline of
simply just continuing training

505
00:22:43,910 --> 00:22:44,970
on a single phase.

506
00:22:44,970 --> 00:22:47,960
And this is noticeably better
than just a randomized mixture

507
00:22:47,960 --> 00:22:50,750
of both phases, as well
as the natural data

508
00:22:50,750 --> 00:22:53,570
distribution compared
to our upsampled data

509
00:22:53,570 --> 00:22:56,000
distribution for Phase 2.

510
00:22:56,000 --> 00:22:59,450
And we also showed that this
is able to scale both on model

511
00:22:59,450 --> 00:23:00,570
scale and data scale.

512
00:23:00,570 --> 00:23:02,862
So if you pull up the token
counts as well as the model

513
00:23:02,862 --> 00:23:05,960
size, we show that
performance further

514
00:23:05,960 --> 00:23:08,030
improves with our two-phase
pretraining compared

515
00:23:08,030 --> 00:23:09,320
to a single phase.

516
00:23:09,320 --> 00:23:11,480
So this highlights
also the effectiveness

517
00:23:11,480 --> 00:23:17,630
of prototyping on smaller
data blends before scaling up.

518
00:23:17,630 --> 00:23:20,390
And furthermore, we
investigated the phase,

519
00:23:20,390 --> 00:23:22,226
the duration of Phase 2.

520
00:23:22,226 --> 00:23:25,680
So should we train on
diverse data for a little bit

521
00:23:25,680 --> 00:23:28,780
and immediately switch to highly
specialized data like math,

522
00:23:28,780 --> 00:23:29,967
or should we wait longer?

523
00:23:29,967 --> 00:23:31,800
And what we found is
performance improves up

524
00:23:31,800 --> 00:23:34,960
to a point around 40% until
there's diminishing returns

525
00:23:34,960 --> 00:23:36,160
likely from overfitting.

526
00:23:36,160 --> 00:23:39,390
Because specialized data,
it's more specialized.

527
00:23:39,390 --> 00:23:41,320
There's typically a
lower number of it,

528
00:23:41,320 --> 00:23:45,040
and it's less diverse compared
to things like web crawl data.

529
00:23:45,040 --> 00:23:49,650
So too much of it can lead
to detrimental or diminishing

530
00:23:49,650 --> 00:23:50,500
returns.

531
00:23:50,500 --> 00:23:53,490
So overall, we see a
well-structured two-phase

532
00:23:53,490 --> 00:23:55,740
pretraining approach, with
careful data selection

533
00:23:55,740 --> 00:23:59,160
and management, is essential
for optimizing LLM performance

534
00:23:59,160 --> 00:24:02,040
while maintaining
scalability and robustness

535
00:24:02,040 --> 00:24:03,760
across different
downstream tasks.

536
00:24:03,760 --> 00:24:06,030
And in case you're
interested, this paper

537
00:24:06,030 --> 00:24:09,930
is also-- preprint
is up on arXiv.

538
00:24:09,930 --> 00:24:12,960
So I guess the overall takeaway
from these two projects

539
00:24:12,960 --> 00:24:15,900
and what I wanted to
get at is on the fact

540
00:24:15,900 --> 00:24:18,820
that data effectiveness
especially for pretraining,

541
00:24:18,820 --> 00:24:20,380
it's not just the
amount of data,

542
00:24:20,380 --> 00:24:22,102
but it's about the
quality of the data,

543
00:24:22,102 --> 00:24:23,560
the ordering and
structure of data,

544
00:24:23,560 --> 00:24:25,660
and how exactly you use it.

545
00:24:25,660 --> 00:24:27,640
So for our first
project, we saw there's

546
00:24:27,640 --> 00:24:31,030
negligible impact of global
order in small-scale training.

547
00:24:31,030 --> 00:24:33,790
But we saw that phase-based
training for larger scales

548
00:24:33,790 --> 00:24:35,000
is highly effective.

549
00:24:35,000 --> 00:24:36,820
And in general,
smart data decisions

550
00:24:36,820 --> 00:24:39,620
are essential for models
to generalize across tasks.

551
00:24:39,620 --> 00:24:42,970
So takeaway is our
research underscores

552
00:24:42,970 --> 00:24:46,310
that effective language modeling
isn't just about amassing data

553
00:24:46,310 --> 00:24:48,190
but about smarter
data organization that

554
00:24:48,190 --> 00:24:51,320
harnesses its structure,
quality, and characteristics.

555
00:24:51,320 --> 00:24:54,500
And by continuing to refine
data-centric approaches,

556
00:24:54,500 --> 00:24:56,470
the future of LLM
training promises

557
00:24:56,470 --> 00:25:01,630
smarter, more efficient,
and highly adaptable models.

558
00:25:01,630 --> 00:25:03,700
So now, we'll be moving
to the second stage

559
00:25:03,700 --> 00:25:06,160
after pretraining
which is post-training,

560
00:25:06,160 --> 00:25:08,650
which Chelsea will talk about.

561
00:25:08,650 --> 00:25:09,320
All right.

562
00:25:09,320 --> 00:25:12,280
So we have a pre-trained
model, now what?

563
00:25:12,280 --> 00:25:16,990
How do we adapt to specific
tasks and different domains?

564
00:25:16,990 --> 00:25:20,770
So some major strategies include
fine-tuning, for instance,

565
00:25:20,770 --> 00:25:23,850
like reinforcement learning
with human feedback

566
00:25:23,850 --> 00:25:27,990
or some prompt-based methods,
or some of RAG architecture

567
00:25:27,990 --> 00:25:29,493
and retrieval-based methods.

568
00:25:29,493 --> 00:25:38,490

569
00:25:38,490 --> 00:25:41,830
So one major approach is called
chain-of-though reasoning.

570
00:25:41,830 --> 00:25:44,220
I'm sure you all have
heard of it by now.

571
00:25:44,220 --> 00:25:48,540
So it's essentially a prompting
technique to think step by step.

572
00:25:48,540 --> 00:25:52,540
So it shows the intermediate
steps, provide guidance,

573
00:25:52,540 --> 00:25:55,960
and this is similar to
the way how humans think.

574
00:25:55,960 --> 00:25:59,880
We can imagine that we
typically break down a problem

575
00:25:59,880 --> 00:26:02,370
into subsequent steps
to help us better

576
00:26:02,370 --> 00:26:04,740
understand the problem itself.

577
00:26:04,740 --> 00:26:06,990
And another benefit
of chain of thought

578
00:26:06,990 --> 00:26:11,040
is that it allows some
of interpretable window

579
00:26:11,040 --> 00:26:13,170
into the behavior of the model.

580
00:26:13,170 --> 00:26:17,160
And this can suggest that there
is more knowledge embedded

581
00:26:17,160 --> 00:26:20,273
in the model's weights than
just prompting a response.

582
00:26:20,273 --> 00:26:25,880

583
00:26:25,880 --> 00:26:28,530
So this here is an example
of chain of thought.

584
00:26:28,530 --> 00:26:31,820
On the left, we have
it solve a problem

585
00:26:31,820 --> 00:26:34,220
in a one-shot manner,
which turns out

586
00:26:34,220 --> 00:26:36,410
to get to the wrong answer.

587
00:26:36,410 --> 00:26:39,200
And on the right over
there, it produces

588
00:26:39,200 --> 00:26:41,010
a sequence of reasoning chains.

589
00:26:41,010 --> 00:26:44,065
And ultimately, it arrives
at the correct answer.

590
00:26:44,065 --> 00:26:51,050

591
00:26:51,050 --> 00:26:54,200
So naturally, this
brings up an extension

592
00:26:54,200 --> 00:26:57,210
of chain-of-thought, which
is called a tree-of-thought.

593
00:26:57,210 --> 00:26:59,040
And this is another
prompting technique.

594
00:26:59,040 --> 00:27:02,060
But instead of producing
a singular reasoning path

595
00:27:02,060 --> 00:27:06,080
as a chain-of-thought does, it
considers multiple reasoning

596
00:27:06,080 --> 00:27:11,030
trajectories and then uses some
sort of self-evaluation process

597
00:27:11,030 --> 00:27:15,750
to decide on the final outputs
such as majority voting.

598
00:27:15,750 --> 00:27:18,470
So in the picture, you can
see that tree-of-thought

599
00:27:18,470 --> 00:27:21,210
generates different
reasoning paths

600
00:27:21,210 --> 00:27:23,360
and selects the
best one at the end.

601
00:27:23,360 --> 00:27:28,710

602
00:27:28,710 --> 00:27:31,660
So another way is through
program-of-thought.

603
00:27:31,660 --> 00:27:34,050
And this basically
generates code

604
00:27:34,050 --> 00:27:36,900
as the intermediate
reasoning steps.

605
00:27:36,900 --> 00:27:38,970
And overall, what
this does is that it

606
00:27:38,970 --> 00:27:42,330
offloads some
problem-solving technique

607
00:27:42,330 --> 00:27:45,850
to some code interpreter.

608
00:27:45,850 --> 00:27:48,450
So it formalizes
language into programs

609
00:27:48,450 --> 00:27:50,903
to arrive at more
precise answers.

610
00:27:50,903 --> 00:27:59,250

611
00:27:59,250 --> 00:28:03,390
So we have seen that this
problem decomposition seems

612
00:28:03,390 --> 00:28:05,730
helpful for different tasks.

613
00:28:05,730 --> 00:28:08,860
So one way is through
Socratic questioning,

614
00:28:08,860 --> 00:28:12,330
which is basically using
a self-questioning module

615
00:28:12,330 --> 00:28:15,210
to propose subproblems
related to the original

616
00:28:15,210 --> 00:28:18,730
and solves those in
a recursive manner.

617
00:28:18,730 --> 00:28:21,970
So for instance, if
the question is like,

618
00:28:21,970 --> 00:28:23,680
what fills the balloons?

619
00:28:23,680 --> 00:28:26,260
This leads to the next
subquestion, which is like,

620
00:28:26,260 --> 00:28:28,010
what can make a balloon float?

621
00:28:28,010 --> 00:28:30,760
And then by decomposing
the original problem

622
00:28:30,760 --> 00:28:36,300
into subsequent problems, it
can better solve at the end.

623
00:28:36,300 --> 00:28:42,820

624
00:28:42,820 --> 00:28:45,850
So finally, another problem
decomposition method

625
00:28:45,850 --> 00:28:48,040
is through computational graphs.

626
00:28:48,040 --> 00:28:50,890
So this basically formulates
compositional tasks

627
00:28:50,890 --> 00:28:54,400
as a computation
graphs by breaking down

628
00:28:54,400 --> 00:28:58,790
the reasoning into different
sub-procedures and nodes.

629
00:28:58,790 --> 00:29:01,630
So the key takeaway here
is that transformers

630
00:29:01,630 --> 00:29:05,170
can solve compositional
tasks by reducing reasoning

631
00:29:05,170 --> 00:29:09,790
into subgraphs, and this
is without developing

632
00:29:09,790 --> 00:29:12,535
some systematic
problem-solving skill.

633
00:29:12,535 --> 00:29:13,035
Right.

634
00:29:13,035 --> 00:29:16,190
So Chelsea touched on
chain-of-thought and everything

635
00:29:16,190 --> 00:29:18,600
that expands upon
it or improves it.

636
00:29:18,600 --> 00:29:21,740
And that's mainly a
prompting-based method

637
00:29:21,740 --> 00:29:23,158
for inference time.

638
00:29:23,158 --> 00:29:24,950
Next, I'll be talking
more at reinforcement

639
00:29:24,950 --> 00:29:27,158
learning and feedback
mechanisms, which are typically

640
00:29:27,158 --> 00:29:30,680
used for things like further
fine-tuning a pre-trained model.

641
00:29:30,680 --> 00:29:32,390
So the most popular
is this thing

642
00:29:32,390 --> 00:29:35,780
called Reinforcement Learning
with Human Feedback or RLHF.

643
00:29:35,780 --> 00:29:38,910
So this trains a reward model
directly from human feedback.

644
00:29:38,910 --> 00:29:41,070
So you do is you take
your pre-trained model,

645
00:29:41,070 --> 00:29:42,930
get it to generate
several responses,

646
00:29:42,930 --> 00:29:45,680
and then you typically take
a pair, a pair of responses

647
00:29:45,680 --> 00:29:48,240
and have humans rate
which one they prefer.

648
00:29:48,240 --> 00:29:51,980
And you can train a reward
model based on this basically

649
00:29:51,980 --> 00:29:54,320
using a reinforcement
learning optimization

650
00:29:54,320 --> 00:29:55,525
algorithm such as PPO.

651
00:29:55,525 --> 00:29:58,580

652
00:29:58,580 --> 00:30:01,700
Now, there's an improvement
to PPO called DPO or Direct

653
00:30:01,700 --> 00:30:03,000
Preference Optimization.

654
00:30:03,000 --> 00:30:04,940
So this more directly
trains the model

655
00:30:04,940 --> 00:30:06,950
to prefer outputs that
humans rank higher

656
00:30:06,950 --> 00:30:09,110
compared to having a
separate reward model, which

657
00:30:09,110 --> 00:30:10,520
is much more efficient.

658
00:30:10,520 --> 00:30:14,120
So basically, you
can think of it

659
00:30:14,120 --> 00:30:17,190
as it more closely ties the
reward directly into the loss

660
00:30:17,190 --> 00:30:21,480
function itself by helping
the LLM to maximize

661
00:30:21,480 --> 00:30:24,270
the likelihood of generating
preferred responses

662
00:30:24,270 --> 00:30:26,790
and minimize the
likelihood of the responses

663
00:30:26,790 --> 00:30:28,760
that humans did not prefer.

664
00:30:28,760 --> 00:30:31,620

665
00:30:31,620 --> 00:30:35,710
And there's a extension to
RLHF, which is called RLAIF.

666
00:30:35,710 --> 00:30:39,240
So this is simply replacing
the human with an AI.

667
00:30:39,240 --> 00:30:42,150
So you typically have
a pretty good LLM

668
00:30:42,150 --> 00:30:45,060
that's able to provide accurate
preference judgments of which

669
00:30:45,060 --> 00:30:46,830
response it prefers.

670
00:30:46,830 --> 00:30:49,350
And this is less
costly basically

671
00:30:49,350 --> 00:30:50,890
compared to human annotators.

672
00:30:50,890 --> 00:30:52,600
And then basically
you do the same thing.

673
00:30:52,600 --> 00:30:57,810
You train a reward model
based on the LLM's preferences

674
00:30:57,810 --> 00:30:58,480
instead.

675
00:30:58,480 --> 00:31:04,320
And actually, human evaluators
found that RLAIF-tuned outputs

676
00:31:04,320 --> 00:31:07,440
were similar to RLHF,
showing that this

677
00:31:07,440 --> 00:31:10,800
is a more scalable and
cost-efficient approach compared

678
00:31:10,800 --> 00:31:11,830
to human feedback.

679
00:31:11,830 --> 00:31:13,610
But there's one
disadvantage here,

680
00:31:13,610 --> 00:31:17,860
which is it really depends on
the capabilities or the accuracy

681
00:31:17,860 --> 00:31:19,750
of judgments of the
LLM you're using

682
00:31:19,750 --> 00:31:22,300
to provide your preferences.

683
00:31:22,300 --> 00:31:27,110
So if you're using one that
is incapable or very noisy,

684
00:31:27,110 --> 00:31:32,260
then that's going to
hurt your post-training.

685
00:31:32,260 --> 00:31:35,350
The next is this
thing very hot now

686
00:31:35,350 --> 00:31:38,740
which was used in
DeepSeek both their R1,

687
00:31:38,740 --> 00:31:41,380
as well as some other
models, like their math ones.

688
00:31:41,380 --> 00:31:46,000
So this is called Group Relative
Policy Optimization or GRPO.

689
00:31:46,000 --> 00:31:49,190
So this is a variant of the
PPO optimization algorithm.

690
00:31:49,190 --> 00:31:53,240
But rather than ranking
simply pairs of responses,

691
00:31:53,240 --> 00:31:58,160
it actually ranks a group of
responses in a different order.

692
00:31:58,160 --> 00:31:59,925
So this provides
richer feedback,

693
00:31:59,925 --> 00:32:01,300
which is more
fine-grained and is

694
00:32:01,300 --> 00:32:06,400
much more efficient compared to
simply ranking pairs of outputs.

695
00:32:06,400 --> 00:32:08,770
So this helps
stabilize training,

696
00:32:08,770 --> 00:32:12,370
which is one reason DeepSeek
is very much more data

697
00:32:12,370 --> 00:32:14,920
and compute efficient.

698
00:32:14,920 --> 00:32:17,830
And also, they saw
that it improves

699
00:32:17,830 --> 00:32:20,775
even things like LLM reasoning
especially on things like math.

700
00:32:20,775 --> 00:32:24,280

701
00:32:24,280 --> 00:32:28,310
There's also been other
variations of RLHF and so forth.

702
00:32:28,310 --> 00:32:31,640
One is this thing called
Kahneman-Tversky Optimization.

703
00:32:31,640 --> 00:32:34,720
Not sure if I'm pronouncing
that correctly, but KTO.

704
00:32:34,720 --> 00:32:38,680
So this modifies the standard
loss function typically used

705
00:32:38,680 --> 00:32:41,410
in post-training
things to account

706
00:32:41,410 --> 00:32:43,700
for human biases such
as loss aversion.

707
00:32:43,700 --> 00:32:47,320
So as humans, we care
more about minimizing

708
00:32:47,320 --> 00:32:51,290
disastrous or negative outcomes
than achieving positive ones.

709
00:32:51,290 --> 00:32:53,540
We're more risk averse
in the most case,

710
00:32:53,540 --> 00:32:55,690
although it's very
dependent on the person.

711
00:32:55,690 --> 00:32:59,260
So they encouraged the AI to
behave in a similar manner

712
00:32:59,260 --> 00:33:03,580
by avoiding negative
outcomes, and this basically

713
00:33:03,580 --> 00:33:05,540
adjusts the training
process to reflect this.

714
00:33:05,540 --> 00:33:08,860
And they showed that this is
able to improve performance

715
00:33:08,860 --> 00:33:12,540
on different tasks, although
it depends on the task.

716
00:33:12,540 --> 00:33:15,290
But overall, it shows
more human-like behavior

717
00:33:15,290 --> 00:33:16,910
on particular tasks.

718
00:33:16,910 --> 00:33:21,320
And these are just a subset
of the RLHF and reinforcement

719
00:33:21,320 --> 00:33:25,640
learning and
feedback-based algorithms.

720
00:33:25,640 --> 00:33:28,430
One I want to touch
upon before I finish off

721
00:33:28,430 --> 00:33:30,470
is this thing
called personalizing

722
00:33:30,470 --> 00:33:33,200
RLHF with the variational
preference learning.

723
00:33:33,200 --> 00:33:36,230
So the authors saw that
different demographics

724
00:33:36,230 --> 00:33:37,620
have different preferences.

725
00:33:37,620 --> 00:33:42,200
So typical RLHF averages
everything together.

726
00:33:42,200 --> 00:33:44,300
So what the authors do is
they introduce a latent

727
00:33:44,300 --> 00:33:46,670
variable for every user
preference profile,

728
00:33:46,670 --> 00:33:48,170
for example, a
different demographic

729
00:33:48,170 --> 00:33:51,000
like children,
adults, and so forth

730
00:33:51,000 --> 00:33:54,650
and trains reward models
conditioned on these latent

731
00:33:54,650 --> 00:33:56,480
vectors or factors.

732
00:33:56,480 --> 00:33:59,220
So this leads to something they
call pluralistic alignment,

733
00:33:59,220 --> 00:34:00,980
which is improving
the reward accuracy

734
00:34:00,980 --> 00:34:03,360
for these particular
demographics or subgroups.

735
00:34:03,360 --> 00:34:06,860
So it enables a single
model to adapt its behavior

736
00:34:06,860 --> 00:34:10,889
to different preference profiles
and different demographics

737
00:34:10,889 --> 00:34:13,578
or groups of people.

738
00:34:13,578 --> 00:34:15,120
And now, I'll hand
it back to Chelsea

739
00:34:15,120 --> 00:34:17,170
to talk about self-improving.

740
00:34:17,170 --> 00:34:19,949

741
00:34:19,949 --> 00:34:20,880
All right.

742
00:34:20,880 --> 00:34:26,010
So yeah, let's talk a little bit
about self-improving AI agents.

743
00:34:26,010 --> 00:34:28,949
So what exactly is an AI agent?

744
00:34:28,949 --> 00:34:33,130
So it's essentially a system
that perceives the environment,

745
00:34:33,130 --> 00:34:37,230
makes decisions and takes
actions towards achieving

746
00:34:37,230 --> 00:34:39,270
some specific goal.

747
00:34:39,270 --> 00:34:43,679
And usually, this goal is given
by the human so for instance

748
00:34:43,679 --> 00:34:48,570
like game-playing, task-solving,
or research assistance.

749
00:34:48,570 --> 00:34:51,280
And there are several
components of an AI agent.

750
00:34:51,280 --> 00:34:54,030
So, one, it's goal-directed.

751
00:34:54,030 --> 00:34:57,900
Two, it can make
its own decisions.

752
00:34:57,900 --> 00:35:01,230
Three, it can act iteratively.

753
00:35:01,230 --> 00:35:04,830
Four, there's usually some
memory component to it

754
00:35:04,830 --> 00:35:07,810
and state tracking
component to it.

755
00:35:07,810 --> 00:35:10,690
And finally, there's
some agents that

756
00:35:10,690 --> 00:35:15,640
can use some tools such as
API calls or function calling.

757
00:35:15,640 --> 00:35:18,450
And finally, it can learn
and adapt on its own.

758
00:35:18,450 --> 00:35:24,705

759
00:35:24,705 --> 00:35:25,220
OK.

760
00:35:25,220 --> 00:35:25,720
Yeah.

761
00:35:25,720 --> 00:35:28,690
So self-improvement,
basically, models

762
00:35:28,690 --> 00:35:33,220
can reflect on their own
outputs leading to iterative

763
00:35:33,220 --> 00:35:35,690
improvements over time.

764
00:35:35,690 --> 00:35:39,400
So this typically
consists of several steps.

765
00:35:39,400 --> 00:35:44,170
There's some reflection of
its own internal states.

766
00:35:44,170 --> 00:35:48,880
There's an explanation of
its own reasoning process.

767
00:35:48,880 --> 00:35:51,890
It can evaluate the
quality of its own outputs.

768
00:35:51,890 --> 00:35:55,480
And finally, it can also
simulate multi-step reasoning

769
00:35:55,480 --> 00:35:55,980
chains.

770
00:35:55,980 --> 00:35:59,410

771
00:35:59,410 --> 00:36:02,600
So one technique is refinement.

772
00:36:02,600 --> 00:36:06,970
So this is where you have some
iterative prompting technique

773
00:36:06,970 --> 00:36:11,350
where an LLM critiques and
improves its own outputs.

774
00:36:11,350 --> 00:36:14,320
So it generates some
initial response

775
00:36:14,320 --> 00:36:16,720
and then refines it over time.

776
00:36:16,720 --> 00:36:22,510
And this uses feedback loops to
enhance the overall performance.

777
00:36:22,510 --> 00:36:26,200
So an example would be
it generates some answer

778
00:36:26,200 --> 00:36:31,010
and then it evaluates itself for
weaknesses and inconsistencies.

779
00:36:31,010 --> 00:36:33,490
And finally, it
refines the response

780
00:36:33,490 --> 00:36:36,015
based on the own
self-critique method.

781
00:36:36,015 --> 00:36:39,250

782
00:36:39,250 --> 00:36:42,830
Another technique is
called self-reflection.

783
00:36:42,830 --> 00:36:46,000
So this is where a model
learns from past mistakes

784
00:36:46,000 --> 00:36:49,880
and adjusts future responses
based on past failures.

785
00:36:49,880 --> 00:36:52,930
So there usually is some
long-term memory component

786
00:36:52,930 --> 00:36:54,070
to this.

787
00:36:54,070 --> 00:36:57,280
And an example would be
like the model first detects

788
00:36:57,280 --> 00:37:00,520
some of weak response
from its own outputs,

789
00:37:00,520 --> 00:37:03,370
and then it reflects
on its own mistakes

790
00:37:03,370 --> 00:37:06,870
and generates some
improved answer to it.

791
00:37:06,870 --> 00:37:11,690
And over multiple iterations,
accuracy and reasoning

792
00:37:11,690 --> 00:37:13,420
should improve over time.

793
00:37:13,420 --> 00:37:17,900

794
00:37:17,900 --> 00:37:21,440
Another technique is called
ReAct, which is essentially

795
00:37:21,440 --> 00:37:26,330
just combining reasoning with
external actions such as API

796
00:37:26,330 --> 00:37:29,270
calls or retrievals
from a database.

797
00:37:29,270 --> 00:37:32,510
And this is basically some model
that can interact dynamically

798
00:37:32,510 --> 00:37:34,160
with its environment.

799
00:37:34,160 --> 00:37:39,410
So it gets feedback from taking
multiple action sequences

800
00:37:39,410 --> 00:37:43,010
and incorporating
that into its outputs.

801
00:37:43,010 --> 00:37:47,060
So for instance, the model
will generate a reasoning plan,

802
00:37:47,060 --> 00:37:51,590
and then it will call some
external tool such as web search

803
00:37:51,590 --> 00:37:54,560
or some API call,
and then this model

804
00:37:54,560 --> 00:37:57,533
incorporates the retrieved
data into its final response.

805
00:37:57,533 --> 00:38:01,700

806
00:38:01,700 --> 00:38:06,360
And finally, this leads us to a
framework called Language Agent

807
00:38:06,360 --> 00:38:07,650
Tree Search.

808
00:38:07,650 --> 00:38:10,170
So basically what
LATS is is that it

809
00:38:10,170 --> 00:38:13,590
extends the ReAct framework to
incorporate multiple planning

810
00:38:13,590 --> 00:38:14,470
pathways.

811
00:38:14,470 --> 00:38:17,610
So you can think this like
analogous to chain-of-thought

812
00:38:17,610 --> 00:38:19,290
versus tree-of-thought.

813
00:38:19,290 --> 00:38:22,560
It gathers feedback
from every path

814
00:38:22,560 --> 00:38:25,410
to improve the future
search process, which

815
00:38:25,410 --> 00:38:29,880
is some verbal reinforcement
learning-inspired technique.

816
00:38:29,880 --> 00:38:32,100
And it uses Monte
Carlo Tree Search

817
00:38:32,100 --> 00:38:34,500
to optimize planning
trajectories,

818
00:38:34,500 --> 00:38:38,790
where in the tree structure,
every node represents a state

819
00:38:38,790 --> 00:38:43,890
and every edge represents an
action that the agent can take.

820
00:38:43,890 --> 00:38:48,180
So an example would be it
generates N best new action

821
00:38:48,180 --> 00:38:52,170
sequences, and then it will just
execute them all in parallel.

822
00:38:52,170 --> 00:38:55,710
Then it will use some
self-reflection technique

823
00:38:55,710 --> 00:38:58,530
to score each one,
and then overall,

824
00:38:58,530 --> 00:39:01,900
just continue exploring
from the best state

825
00:39:01,900 --> 00:39:04,620
and update the probabilities
of the past node.

826
00:39:04,620 --> 00:39:07,960

827
00:39:07,960 --> 00:39:10,180
And yeah.

828
00:39:10,180 --> 00:39:11,710
All right.

829
00:39:11,710 --> 00:39:14,440
Next, I'll be talking about
a few other applications

830
00:39:14,440 --> 00:39:16,930
of transformers
outside of language.

831
00:39:16,930 --> 00:39:19,480
I'll start with Vision
Transformers which

832
00:39:19,480 --> 00:39:22,150
have taken vision by storm.

833
00:39:22,150 --> 00:39:26,450
The methodology here is that--

834
00:39:26,450 --> 00:39:29,910
so as I talked about
transformers taking sequences,

835
00:39:29,910 --> 00:39:32,210
but images aren't sequences.

836
00:39:32,210 --> 00:39:36,070
However, what the authors of
the ViT paper came up with

837
00:39:36,070 --> 00:39:40,930
was to split an image up
into patches, which can then

838
00:39:40,930 --> 00:39:43,660
be embedded to form a sequence.

839
00:39:43,660 --> 00:39:45,700
Passing this through
a simple transformer

840
00:39:45,700 --> 00:39:49,640
yielded very good results, for
instance, on classification,

841
00:39:49,640 --> 00:39:52,960
just by adding an
MLP head to the end.

842
00:39:52,960 --> 00:39:56,950
You might ask why apply
transformers to this problem

843
00:39:56,950 --> 00:40:01,160
when CNNs are such a
mainstay in the field.

844
00:40:01,160 --> 00:40:05,060
The main reason is that when
you have a very large data set,

845
00:40:05,060 --> 00:40:07,200
say, in the tens of
millions of examples,

846
00:40:07,200 --> 00:40:10,670
transformers bring in
less inductive biases.

847
00:40:10,670 --> 00:40:14,790
CNNs assume locality and that
pixels are grouped together.

848
00:40:14,790 --> 00:40:18,260
Whereas with transformers
and treating your images

849
00:40:18,260 --> 00:40:20,540
as sequences, you can
see better results

850
00:40:20,540 --> 00:40:24,380
when you have enough
data to train them.

851
00:40:24,380 --> 00:40:28,550
One common architecture
that was impacted by this

852
00:40:28,550 --> 00:40:33,500
was CLIP, which uses ViTs
for its image encoders.

853
00:40:33,500 --> 00:40:37,490
This is the basis of
models like GPT-4o

854
00:40:37,490 --> 00:40:42,140
or other vision-language
models and essentially works

855
00:40:42,140 --> 00:40:43,710
through contrastive learning.

856
00:40:43,710 --> 00:40:48,350
So you take a data set of
paired images and text pairs,

857
00:40:48,350 --> 00:40:52,610
and you train your model
to align the encoder

858
00:40:52,610 --> 00:40:54,030
representations of both.

859
00:40:54,030 --> 00:40:58,620
So if you have an image of
a cat and and the word cat,

860
00:40:58,620 --> 00:41:02,960
then you can learn to
align those embeddings.

861
00:41:02,960 --> 00:41:06,050
And like I mentioned, these have
been applied to vision-language

862
00:41:06,050 --> 00:41:09,710
models like GPT-4 or 4o.

863
00:41:09,710 --> 00:41:15,080
The way these are trained is you
concatenate your encoded image

864
00:41:15,080 --> 00:41:18,740
and text, and you can
train in different stages

865
00:41:18,740 --> 00:41:21,440
such that your model
learns to take both

866
00:41:21,440 --> 00:41:23,660
and to account
for its responses.

867
00:41:23,660 --> 00:41:29,070
And these have done very
well on benchmarks and tasks

868
00:41:29,070 --> 00:41:34,730
for instance like test
questions like I've shown here.

869
00:41:34,730 --> 00:41:38,160
Next, I'll talk about a bit
of my work and neuroscience,

870
00:41:38,160 --> 00:41:42,650
which applies ViTs to
other kinds of data.

871
00:41:42,650 --> 00:41:46,580
So a mainstay in my field is
functional magnetic resonance

872
00:41:46,580 --> 00:41:48,230
imaging, or fMRI.

873
00:41:48,230 --> 00:41:51,140
Essentially, this captures
the amount of oxygen

874
00:41:51,140 --> 00:41:55,070
that each voxel part of your
brain is using at a given point,

875
00:41:55,070 --> 00:41:59,490
and this provides a very
detailed proxy for the activity

876
00:41:59,490 --> 00:42:00,930
going on in your brain.

877
00:42:00,930 --> 00:42:05,190
It can be used to diagnose
diseases and capture

878
00:42:05,190 --> 00:42:10,380
various amounts of data for
better cognitive understanding.

879
00:42:10,380 --> 00:42:12,730
However, this is very
high dimensional.

880
00:42:12,730 --> 00:42:16,810
You might have a million or so
voxels or 100,000 in the brain.

881
00:42:16,810 --> 00:42:20,550
So the first step to using this
data with transformer models

882
00:42:20,550 --> 00:42:24,210
is usually averaging
across well-known regions

883
00:42:24,210 --> 00:42:26,020
or just grouping
together voxels,

884
00:42:26,020 --> 00:42:30,360
and this gives you a more
computationally tractable number

885
00:42:30,360 --> 00:42:33,780
of parcels that
you can train on.

886
00:42:33,780 --> 00:42:37,770
A traditional tool in this field
was just to use linear pairwise

887
00:42:37,770 --> 00:42:42,270
correlation maps, and just these
were enough to get pretty good

888
00:42:42,270 --> 00:42:44,530
diagnoses of things
like Parkinson's.

889
00:42:44,530 --> 00:42:49,180
However, with the advent of tons
of computer vision techniques,

890
00:42:49,180 --> 00:42:51,330
we can apply larger
and more sophisticated

891
00:42:51,330 --> 00:42:54,420
models to these tasks.

892
00:42:54,420 --> 00:42:57,850
One cool, large body
of work in this area

893
00:42:57,850 --> 00:43:02,240
is divvying up the brain into
different functional networks,

894
00:43:02,240 --> 00:43:06,490
so let's say like your vision
system or your daydreaming

895
00:43:06,490 --> 00:43:09,160
network or control, et cetera.

896
00:43:09,160 --> 00:43:14,770
And I'll get into how we
use this to guide our work.

897
00:43:14,770 --> 00:43:16,900
So like I mentioned,
early ML models

898
00:43:16,900 --> 00:43:19,880
just took like linear
correlation maps

899
00:43:19,880 --> 00:43:22,780
so making lots of
assumptions about the data

900
00:43:22,780 --> 00:43:26,740
and just applied typical
neural networks to the task

901
00:43:26,740 --> 00:43:30,200
for regression or classification
tasks or, in some cases,

902
00:43:30,200 --> 00:43:33,400
graph-based analyses
to try to get a deeper

903
00:43:33,400 --> 00:43:36,340
understanding of how the
different parts of the brain

904
00:43:36,340 --> 00:43:39,100
interact with each other.

905
00:43:39,100 --> 00:43:42,520
With computer vision,
we can take our raw data

906
00:43:42,520 --> 00:43:45,200
and just throw that at
a transformer model,

907
00:43:45,200 --> 00:43:49,070
and that does very well as
a pre-training objective.

908
00:43:49,070 --> 00:43:52,930
So what we do is let's say
we have some number of ROIs

909
00:43:52,930 --> 00:43:58,490
across time, we can just mask
out some portion of that data,

910
00:43:58,490 --> 00:44:01,470
pass the rest of the data
through a transformer model,

911
00:44:01,470 --> 00:44:03,450
and have it predict
this portion.

912
00:44:03,450 --> 00:44:07,950
You repeat this across a large
data set and all of your ROIs,

913
00:44:07,950 --> 00:44:10,520
and this provides a very
good self-supervised training

914
00:44:10,520 --> 00:44:12,480
objective for this task.

915
00:44:12,480 --> 00:44:15,080
So self-supervised
essentially means that there

916
00:44:15,080 --> 00:44:17,540
is no paired labeled data here.

917
00:44:17,540 --> 00:44:20,300
We are essentially
just using our raw data

918
00:44:20,300 --> 00:44:22,940
and posing our
objective such that we

919
00:44:22,940 --> 00:44:24,385
can learn directly off of it.

920
00:44:24,385 --> 00:44:27,140

921
00:44:27,140 --> 00:44:30,770
Once you've trained
this model, you

922
00:44:30,770 --> 00:44:33,440
have these dense representations
inside the model that

923
00:44:33,440 --> 00:44:36,120
can be applied downstream
to various tasks

924
00:44:36,120 --> 00:44:38,090
like predicting
patient attributes

925
00:44:38,090 --> 00:44:39,630
or their risk of disease.

926
00:44:39,630 --> 00:44:41,240
And you can also
look at the weights

927
00:44:41,240 --> 00:44:49,760
that your model has learned
to analyses of brain networks.

928
00:44:49,760 --> 00:44:52,590
So in brief, our
approach essentially

929
00:44:52,590 --> 00:44:55,750
consists of taking the
activity in the entire brain,

930
00:44:55,750 --> 00:44:57,850
partitioning out
some small region,

931
00:44:57,850 --> 00:45:00,330
let's say it's
your vision system.

932
00:45:00,330 --> 00:45:04,680
You pass the unmasked portion
into a transformer model, which

933
00:45:04,680 --> 00:45:07,420
learns to predict
the masked portion,

934
00:45:07,420 --> 00:45:09,510
and you can compare this
to your ground truth

935
00:45:09,510 --> 00:45:11,920
to provide your
training objective.

936
00:45:11,920 --> 00:45:16,620

937
00:45:16,620 --> 00:45:19,360
One key thing we use
here is cross-attention.

938
00:45:19,360 --> 00:45:21,570
So what we talked about
before with language

939
00:45:21,570 --> 00:45:23,370
was self-attention,
wherein you are

940
00:45:23,370 --> 00:45:27,240
attending to the current
sequence you're looking at.

941
00:45:27,240 --> 00:45:30,735
In cross-attention, you have
two different sequences.

942
00:45:30,735 --> 00:45:32,610
Let's say in machine
translation you have one

943
00:45:32,610 --> 00:45:34,530
in English and in French.

944
00:45:34,530 --> 00:45:37,320
And essentially,
you apply attention

945
00:45:37,320 --> 00:45:39,960
between the two
sequences instead

946
00:45:39,960 --> 00:45:43,740
of just on a single sequence.

947
00:45:43,740 --> 00:45:47,760
So our most basic architecture
takes advantage of this

948
00:45:47,760 --> 00:45:50,860
through just a singular
cross-attention decoder.

949
00:45:50,860 --> 00:45:54,380
Having a very small model makes
for better interpretability.

950
00:45:54,380 --> 00:45:56,110
And like I mentioned,
this model just

951
00:45:56,110 --> 00:45:59,980
learns to predict masked brain
regions from unmasked ones.

952
00:45:59,980 --> 00:46:02,410
Once we've done this, we can
analyze again the attention

953
00:46:02,410 --> 00:46:05,980
weights to gain a deeper
understanding of networks

954
00:46:05,980 --> 00:46:09,220
and also apply this
to downstream tasks.

955
00:46:09,220 --> 00:46:11,020
So some modeling results.

956
00:46:11,020 --> 00:46:13,930
Here, I've plotted
like the brain activity

957
00:46:13,930 --> 00:46:15,230
from different patients.

958
00:46:15,230 --> 00:46:17,920
And you can see that the
model does pretty well

959
00:46:17,920 --> 00:46:21,940
in matching the ground
truth for two networks

960
00:46:21,940 --> 00:46:24,370
that I've shown here, the
salience network, which

961
00:46:24,370 --> 00:46:28,010
is involved in your senses
and decision making,

962
00:46:28,010 --> 00:46:31,180
and the Default Mode
Network or DMN, which

963
00:46:31,180 --> 00:46:35,110
is responsible for daydreaming
or just recapitulating

964
00:46:35,110 --> 00:46:38,860
your brain information when
you're not doing a certain task.

965
00:46:38,860 --> 00:46:41,740
On the bottom, we have
the attention weights

966
00:46:41,740 --> 00:46:46,220
for this model, which I've split
up by all of the other networks.

967
00:46:46,220 --> 00:46:48,160
So for instance,
on the left, when

968
00:46:48,160 --> 00:46:51,430
predicting the salience network,
we can see from our model

969
00:46:51,430 --> 00:46:54,700
that it is heavily dependent
on the default mode and control

970
00:46:54,700 --> 00:46:55,430
networks.

971
00:46:55,430 --> 00:46:57,520
So this gives us a
better understanding

972
00:46:57,520 --> 00:47:01,250
of how different brain networks
are connected to each other

973
00:47:01,250 --> 00:47:05,080
or how they might share
information inside the brain.

974
00:47:05,080 --> 00:47:08,930
For other networks, though, like
vision, these are more singular.

975
00:47:08,930 --> 00:47:10,660
We can't predict them very well.

976
00:47:10,660 --> 00:47:13,820
Or subcortical regions, say,
those involved in memory,

977
00:47:13,820 --> 00:47:15,300
we also cannot
predict very well.

978
00:47:15,300 --> 00:47:18,610

979
00:47:18,610 --> 00:47:20,600
So this is all well and cool.

980
00:47:20,600 --> 00:47:22,660
We can predict brain
activity, but what can

981
00:47:22,660 --> 00:47:24,700
we do with this model?

982
00:47:24,700 --> 00:47:28,000
If we simply replace
one component

983
00:47:28,000 --> 00:47:31,390
of the model with a learnable
token, which corresponds

984
00:47:31,390 --> 00:47:33,440
to predicting
Parkinson's disease,

985
00:47:33,440 --> 00:47:38,900
then we can use this model
to predict that ailment.

986
00:47:38,900 --> 00:47:41,590
So if you look on the right,
after some fine tuning

987
00:47:41,590 --> 00:47:44,710
on a labeled data set, we
can see some clustering

988
00:47:44,710 --> 00:47:48,440
in the model's embedding,
which corresponds

989
00:47:48,440 --> 00:47:51,590
to getting close to 70%
accuracy in predicting

990
00:47:51,590 --> 00:47:55,875
this disease, which is
much higher than using

991
00:47:55,875 --> 00:47:58,250
the correlation-based based
methods or linear assumptions

992
00:47:58,250 --> 00:47:59,417
that I talked about earlier.

993
00:47:59,417 --> 00:48:02,990

994
00:48:02,990 --> 00:48:03,720
All right.

995
00:48:03,720 --> 00:48:06,530
So now that we have some
background on these transformer

996
00:48:06,530 --> 00:48:08,790
models and a couple
of their applications,

997
00:48:08,790 --> 00:48:11,340
let's talk about the
future and what's next.

998
00:48:11,340 --> 00:48:13,670
So overall, these
transformer models

999
00:48:13,670 --> 00:48:16,010
can enable a lot
more applications

1000
00:48:16,010 --> 00:48:17,970
across every
industry and sector.

1001
00:48:17,970 --> 00:48:21,200
This includes generalist
agents as well as longer video

1002
00:48:21,200 --> 00:48:24,530
understanding and generation
across the finance and business

1003
00:48:24,530 --> 00:48:27,660
sector, domain-specific
foundation models like,

1004
00:48:27,660 --> 00:48:31,910
for example, one could imagine
a DoctorGPT or a LawyerGPT

1005
00:48:31,910 --> 00:48:36,740
or an "insert field" GPT as well
as potential real-world impacts

1006
00:48:36,740 --> 00:48:39,780
like personalized education
and tutoring systems,

1007
00:48:39,780 --> 00:48:43,070
advanced healthcare diagnostics,
environmental monitoring

1008
00:48:43,070 --> 00:48:46,470
and protection, real-time
multilingual communication

1009
00:48:46,470 --> 00:48:49,330
as well as in interactive
environment and gaming,

1010
00:48:49,330 --> 00:48:51,306
for example,
non-playable characters.

1011
00:48:51,306 --> 00:48:55,890

1012
00:48:55,890 --> 00:48:57,310
What is missing though?

1013
00:48:57,310 --> 00:49:00,120
What information might
we need and what can we

1014
00:49:00,120 --> 00:49:01,420
develop in the future?

1015
00:49:01,420 --> 00:49:07,030
Currently, we're missing
reducing computation complexity,

1016
00:49:07,030 --> 00:49:11,430
enhancing human controllability,
alignment with the language

1017
00:49:11,430 --> 00:49:14,430
models of the human
brain, adaptive learning

1018
00:49:14,430 --> 00:49:18,360
and generalization across
different domains, and finally,

1019
00:49:18,360 --> 00:49:21,450
multi-sensory, multimodal
embodiment like intuitive

1020
00:49:21,450 --> 00:49:23,110
physics and commonsense.

1021
00:49:23,110 --> 00:49:26,490
So one might consider
these barriers

1022
00:49:26,490 --> 00:49:28,990
to developing artificial
general intelligence,

1023
00:49:28,990 --> 00:49:30,840
and these are some
of the limitations

1024
00:49:30,840 --> 00:49:32,210
of current transformer models.

1025
00:49:32,210 --> 00:49:34,703

1026
00:49:34,703 --> 00:49:36,120
Some other things
that are missing

1027
00:49:36,120 --> 00:49:38,400
include infinite
and external memory,

1028
00:49:38,400 --> 00:49:41,460
like Neural Turing Machines,
infinite self-improvement

1029
00:49:41,460 --> 00:49:44,140
capabilities like continual
or lifelong learning.

1030
00:49:44,140 --> 00:49:46,740
This is another central
tenet of human learning

1031
00:49:46,740 --> 00:49:49,020
that we're not able to
replicate at the moment.

1032
00:49:49,020 --> 00:49:52,900
Complete autonomy, including
curiosity, desires, and goals,

1033
00:49:52,900 --> 00:49:55,920
and long-horizon
decision-making, as well as

1034
00:49:55,920 --> 00:49:58,210
emotional intelligence,
social understanding,

1035
00:49:58,210 --> 00:50:01,146
and of course, ethical
reasoning and value alignment.

1036
00:50:01,146 --> 00:50:05,380

1037
00:50:05,380 --> 00:50:05,880
All right.

1038
00:50:05,880 --> 00:50:09,390
So there's still a plethora
of remaining weaknesses

1039
00:50:09,390 --> 00:50:12,690
or challenges around
transformers, large language

1040
00:50:12,690 --> 00:50:15,550
models, and AI in
general these days,

1041
00:50:15,550 --> 00:50:17,980
so I'll touch upon a
couple of them briefly.

1042
00:50:17,980 --> 00:50:21,210
The first is, like I mentioned
earlier, efficiency, being

1043
00:50:21,210 --> 00:50:25,080
able to minify or have tiny
LLMs or models that you

1044
00:50:25,080 --> 00:50:29,370
can run on your phone, on
your smartwatch, et cetera.

1045
00:50:29,370 --> 00:50:31,830
So that's a big
trend these days is

1046
00:50:31,830 --> 00:50:35,700
using LLMs for everyday
applications and purposes.

1047
00:50:35,700 --> 00:50:38,700
And again, you want to be able
to run them quickly and easily

1048
00:50:38,700 --> 00:50:40,830
on smaller devices.

1049
00:50:40,830 --> 00:50:42,210
Right now, there
is more and more

1050
00:50:42,210 --> 00:50:43,810
work on smaller
and more efficient

1051
00:50:43,810 --> 00:50:48,220
open-source models, things like
DeepSeek, LLaMA and Mistral,

1052
00:50:48,220 --> 00:50:51,140
but they're still somewhat
large and a bit expensive

1053
00:50:51,140 --> 00:50:53,253
especially if you're
looking to finetune.

1054
00:50:53,253 --> 00:50:55,420
They're still not accessible
to everybody especially

1055
00:50:55,420 --> 00:50:57,260
on smaller devices.

1056
00:50:57,260 --> 00:50:59,080
So in the future,
again, we want to aim

1057
00:50:59,080 --> 00:51:02,860
to have the ability to finetune
or run these models locally

1058
00:51:02,860 --> 00:51:06,070
on whatever device you want.

1059
00:51:06,070 --> 00:51:10,930
The second is as our models,
as our LLMs scale up,

1060
00:51:10,930 --> 00:51:14,320
trillions of parameters
trained on trillions of tokens

1061
00:51:14,320 --> 00:51:16,120
across the internet,
what happens

1062
00:51:16,120 --> 00:51:17,950
is this makes it a
huge black-box that

1063
00:51:17,950 --> 00:51:20,110
is difficult to
understand or interpret.

1064
00:51:20,110 --> 00:51:22,660
It's hard to know what exactly
is going on behind the scenes.

1065
00:51:22,660 --> 00:51:25,330
When you ask it to solve
XYZ, and it comes up

1066
00:51:25,330 --> 00:51:27,680
with answers ABC, how
exactly did it get there?

1067
00:51:27,680 --> 00:51:29,750
Why did it choose
those answers instead?

1068
00:51:29,750 --> 00:51:31,420
And so forth.

1069
00:51:31,420 --> 00:51:34,870
So more work on interpretability
for LLMs will give us

1070
00:51:34,870 --> 00:51:37,400
a better idea of what
or how to improve them,

1071
00:51:37,400 --> 00:51:41,110
easier ways of controlling
them, and better

1072
00:51:41,110 --> 00:51:43,830
alignment, for example,
being able to prevent them

1073
00:51:43,830 --> 00:51:45,300
from producing
certain outputs that

1074
00:51:45,300 --> 00:51:48,667
might be unsafe or unethical.

1075
00:51:48,667 --> 00:51:51,000
So there's this area, which
has gotten even more popular

1076
00:51:51,000 --> 00:51:53,370
recently called mechanistic
interpretability, which

1077
00:51:53,370 --> 00:51:55,650
is trying to understand
how individual components

1078
00:51:55,650 --> 00:51:59,580
or operations, even sometimes
down to the individual node

1079
00:51:59,580 --> 00:52:03,070
level, so very
granular, in an ML model

1080
00:52:03,070 --> 00:52:05,250
contribute to its overall
decision-making process

1081
00:52:05,250 --> 00:52:09,300
with the goal, again, of
unpacking this black-box

1082
00:52:09,300 --> 00:52:14,460
for clear insight on how exactly
they work behind the scenes.

1083
00:52:14,460 --> 00:52:18,060
Next, I feel like
we're approaching

1084
00:52:18,060 --> 00:52:20,850
or we're already seeing
diminishing returns

1085
00:52:20,850 --> 00:52:22,270
with simply scaling up.

1086
00:52:22,270 --> 00:52:25,560
So larger models on
more data does not

1087
00:52:25,560 --> 00:52:27,460
seem to be the be-all,
end-all solution.

1088
00:52:27,460 --> 00:52:30,420
So one-size-fits-all and frozen
pre-trained models have already

1089
00:52:30,420 --> 00:52:32,730
started, leading to
diminishing returns.

1090
00:52:32,730 --> 00:52:34,210
So again, pre-training
performance.

1091
00:52:34,210 --> 00:52:40,810
So the first half of training
LLMs, it's likely saturating.

1092
00:52:40,810 --> 00:52:43,210
Hence, there's been more focus
on post-training methods.

1093
00:52:43,210 --> 00:52:46,000
Everything we've talked about,
feedback and RL mechanisms,

1094
00:52:46,000 --> 00:52:48,150
and prompting methods
like chain-of-thought,

1095
00:52:48,150 --> 00:52:50,410
self-improvement and
refinement and so forth.

1096
00:52:50,410 --> 00:52:52,578
However, all of these
post-training mechanisms

1097
00:52:52,578 --> 00:52:54,120
are going to be
fundamentally limited

1098
00:52:54,120 --> 00:52:57,550
by the overall performance or
capabilities of the base model.

1099
00:52:57,550 --> 00:53:00,270
So you can argue that the
pre-training is fundamentally

1100
00:53:00,270 --> 00:53:03,630
what gives the basis or
the foundational knowledge

1101
00:53:03,630 --> 00:53:05,400
and capabilities to the model.

1102
00:53:05,400 --> 00:53:07,920
So we should not just stop
investigating pre-training

1103
00:53:07,920 --> 00:53:10,380
just because we're
hitting scaling limits.

1104
00:53:10,380 --> 00:53:12,300
Furthermore, too
much post-training

1105
00:53:12,300 --> 00:53:13,980
can actually lead to an issue.

1106
00:53:13,980 --> 00:53:17,250
This is called
catastrophic forgetting

1107
00:53:17,250 --> 00:53:19,870
where the model forgets stuff
it's learned beforehand.

1108
00:53:19,870 --> 00:53:22,320
For example, during
pre-training, because you're

1109
00:53:22,320 --> 00:53:24,900
overloading it with
tons of new information

1110
00:53:24,900 --> 00:53:28,390
in a new domain or a new
task during post-training.

1111
00:53:28,390 --> 00:53:32,270
So how do we break through
this scaling law limit?

1112
00:53:32,270 --> 00:53:35,460

1113
00:53:35,460 --> 00:53:38,670
Some potential
things to investigate

1114
00:53:38,670 --> 00:53:40,380
would be new architectures.

1115
00:53:40,380 --> 00:53:44,630
There are different things like
Mamba, state space machines,

1116
00:53:44,630 --> 00:53:46,730
those architectures,
and it would

1117
00:53:46,730 --> 00:53:48,800
be good to see more
investigation on even

1118
00:53:48,800 --> 00:53:50,690
non-transformer
architectures, which

1119
00:53:50,690 --> 00:53:54,230
is a bit ironic considering this
class is transformers united,

1120
00:53:54,230 --> 00:53:56,960
but we always encourage
more diversity

1121
00:53:56,960 --> 00:53:58,830
and thinking outside the box.

1122
00:53:58,830 --> 00:54:02,150
Also again, everything I
talked about high-quality data

1123
00:54:02,150 --> 00:54:04,850
and smart data ordering
and structuring

1124
00:54:04,850 --> 00:54:07,430
strategies and overall
improved training

1125
00:54:07,430 --> 00:54:11,100
procedures, improved
algorithms, loss functions,

1126
00:54:11,100 --> 00:54:14,090
optimization algorithms
and so forth.

1127
00:54:14,090 --> 00:54:16,490
Another goal, as we've
mentioned several times,

1128
00:54:16,490 --> 00:54:20,120
is to be able to bring these
advanced capabilities to smaller

1129
00:54:20,120 --> 00:54:21,530
models.

1130
00:54:21,530 --> 00:54:23,810
Furthermore, we
would still encourage

1131
00:54:23,810 --> 00:54:26,490
more theoretical and
interpretability research

1132
00:54:26,490 --> 00:54:28,160
including things like
cognitive science

1133
00:54:28,160 --> 00:54:30,680
and neuroscience-inspired
work, which

1134
00:54:30,680 --> 00:54:33,260
Karan and I have talked
about some of that

1135
00:54:33,260 --> 00:54:34,970
that we've done recently.

1136
00:54:34,970 --> 00:54:38,360
And so the next step will be
models that are not just larger,

1137
00:54:38,360 --> 00:54:40,776
but ones that are more
smarter and more adaptable.

1138
00:54:40,776 --> 00:54:43,390

1139
00:54:43,390 --> 00:54:48,010
So again, there's this one
major thing or major weakness

1140
00:54:48,010 --> 00:54:52,990
that I think still
bridges the gap between AI

1141
00:54:52,990 --> 00:54:57,130
and humans, which is continual
or lifelong learning so

1142
00:54:57,130 --> 00:55:00,040
AI systems that can
continuously improve by learning

1143
00:55:00,040 --> 00:55:02,453
after deployment,
after being pre-trained

1144
00:55:02,453 --> 00:55:04,120
using implicit feedback,
real-word world

1145
00:55:04,120 --> 00:55:05,750
experience, and so forth.

1146
00:55:05,750 --> 00:55:08,410
So essentially, this is
infinite and permanent

1147
00:55:08,410 --> 00:55:10,340
fundamental self-improvement.

1148
00:55:10,340 --> 00:55:12,230
We're not just talking
about RAG or retrieval

1149
00:55:12,230 --> 00:55:14,410
like putting knowledge
in a retrieval database

1150
00:55:14,410 --> 00:55:16,580
that you can
retrieve at test time

1151
00:55:16,580 --> 00:55:19,510
but updating the brain or
the weights of the model

1152
00:55:19,510 --> 00:55:20,540
continuously.

1153
00:55:20,540 --> 00:55:21,953
So this is similar to us.

1154
00:55:21,953 --> 00:55:23,120
So we're learning every day.

1155
00:55:23,120 --> 00:55:25,270
I'm learning right
now by talking to you.

1156
00:55:25,270 --> 00:55:27,392
I learn every time I
talk to somebody else

1157
00:55:27,392 --> 00:55:28,850
as I'm going through
my daily life.

1158
00:55:28,850 --> 00:55:31,190
But these models, after
they're frozen or pre-trained,

1159
00:55:31,190 --> 00:55:32,560
that doesn't really happen.

1160
00:55:32,560 --> 00:55:35,620
They only way they truly learn
or their brain or weights are

1161
00:55:35,620 --> 00:55:37,443
updated is through finetuning.

1162
00:55:37,443 --> 00:55:38,610
And again, we don't do that.

1163
00:55:38,610 --> 00:55:40,360
We don't sit-in a chair
every three months

1164
00:55:40,360 --> 00:55:42,620
and have someone reread
the internet to us

1165
00:55:42,620 --> 00:55:45,740
or something like that.

1166
00:55:45,740 --> 00:55:48,652
So again, this is
almost wasted work.

1167
00:55:48,652 --> 00:55:50,360
So currently during
inference, the models

1168
00:55:50,360 --> 00:55:52,280
are not actually learning
and updating their weights.

1169
00:55:52,280 --> 00:55:54,570
When they're talking, when
ChatGPT is talking to you,

1170
00:55:54,570 --> 00:55:58,250
it's not truly updating
its brain or weights.

1171
00:55:58,250 --> 00:56:01,610
So this is a very challenging
problem but in our opinion

1172
00:56:01,610 --> 00:56:04,640
is likely one of the
keys potentially to AGI

1173
00:56:04,640 --> 00:56:07,700
or truly human-like AI systems.

1174
00:56:07,700 --> 00:56:11,190
So there's different current
work that tries to tackle this.

1175
00:56:11,190 --> 00:56:13,760
There's things like finetuning
a smaller model based

1176
00:56:13,760 --> 00:56:17,780
on traces from a larger model,
things like model distillation

1177
00:56:17,780 --> 00:56:21,110
related to a lot of things
like improvement and so forth.

1178
00:56:21,110 --> 00:56:24,650
But this is, again, not
truly continual learning.

1179
00:56:24,650 --> 00:56:27,650
So some questions are, what
mechanisms could potentially

1180
00:56:27,650 --> 00:56:31,380
truly enable real
lifelong learning

1181
00:56:31,380 --> 00:56:33,180
and will this be
gradient updates?

1182
00:56:33,180 --> 00:56:34,828
So actually updating the brain.

1183
00:56:34,828 --> 00:56:36,870
Will it be things like
targeting particular nodes

1184
00:56:36,870 --> 00:56:37,900
in the architecture?

1185
00:56:37,900 --> 00:56:41,820
Will it be having things like
particular memory architectures

1186
00:56:41,820 --> 00:56:44,850
or different parts of the
neural network solely focused

1187
00:56:44,850 --> 00:56:48,150
on continuous updates and
learning or even things

1188
00:56:48,150 --> 00:56:50,100
meta-learning learning
and looking more

1189
00:56:50,100 --> 00:56:53,950
at the broad scale of
things or the broader scope?

1190
00:56:53,950 --> 00:56:57,540
So one line of work which
has seen a bit of traction

1191
00:56:57,540 --> 00:56:58,720
is model editing.

1192
00:56:58,720 --> 00:57:01,840
So this is related to work on
mechanistic interpretability.

1193
00:57:01,840 --> 00:57:04,480
So instead of updating
the whole model,

1194
00:57:04,480 --> 00:57:06,550
if we're given a new
fact or a new data point,

1195
00:57:06,550 --> 00:57:10,170
can we target specific nodes
or neurons in the model

1196
00:57:10,170 --> 00:57:11,880
that we should update?

1197
00:57:11,880 --> 00:57:15,720
So one work called Rank-One
Model Editing or ROME

1198
00:57:15,720 --> 00:57:19,530
tries to do this through
causal intervention mechanisms

1199
00:57:19,530 --> 00:57:23,010
to determine which
neuron activations

1200
00:57:23,010 --> 00:57:25,740
most correspond to particular
factual predictions

1201
00:57:25,740 --> 00:57:27,850
and then updating
them appropriately.

1202
00:57:27,850 --> 00:57:31,900
But as you can possibly suspect,
this has a lot of weaknesses.

1203
00:57:31,900 --> 00:57:35,140
So firstly, this works mainly
for knowledge-based things

1204
00:57:35,140 --> 00:57:36,110
or simple facts.

1205
00:57:36,110 --> 00:57:38,770
What if we want to
update the actual skills

1206
00:57:38,770 --> 00:57:40,040
or capabilities of a model?

1207
00:57:40,040 --> 00:57:42,440
We want it to be better
at math in general.

1208
00:57:42,440 --> 00:57:45,440
We want it to be better at
advanced analogical reasoning,

1209
00:57:45,440 --> 00:57:46,750
unlike humans.

1210
00:57:46,750 --> 00:57:48,910
Then something
like model editing

1211
00:57:48,910 --> 00:57:52,460
based on factual predictions
doesn't seem like it'll work.

1212
00:57:52,460 --> 00:57:55,850
The second is these are
targeting one fact at a time,

1213
00:57:55,850 --> 00:58:00,580
so it's not easy to propagate
these changes to other nodes

1214
00:58:00,580 --> 00:58:02,390
based on related facts.

1215
00:58:02,390 --> 00:58:08,320
For example, let's
say someone's mother,

1216
00:58:08,320 --> 00:58:10,130
we want to update
a fact about them,

1217
00:58:10,130 --> 00:58:11,710
then we should
also update a fact

1218
00:58:11,710 --> 00:58:14,530
about that person's
brother, because they

1219
00:58:14,530 --> 00:58:16,090
have the same mother.

1220
00:58:16,090 --> 00:58:18,460
But this approach
will only update it

1221
00:58:18,460 --> 00:58:20,570
for the original
person in question,

1222
00:58:20,570 --> 00:58:22,850
but not any of the relatives.

1223
00:58:22,850 --> 00:58:24,690
So this is just one example.

1224
00:58:24,690 --> 00:58:27,190
So there's a lot of other works
which have spun out recently

1225
00:58:27,190 --> 00:58:30,310
in continual learning, which
is good that this area has

1226
00:58:30,310 --> 00:58:31,520
seen more work.

1227
00:58:31,520 --> 00:58:35,690
So I will very briefly
describe some of these.

1228
00:58:35,690 --> 00:58:38,360
One is this thing called MEMIT,
which is directly related

1229
00:58:38,360 --> 00:58:41,905
to what I just said about
ROME, but it's mass editing

1230
00:58:41,905 --> 00:58:42,780
of factual knowledge.

1231
00:58:42,780 --> 00:58:45,750
So instead of a simple
fact or memory at a time,

1232
00:58:45,750 --> 00:58:48,740
it's able to simultaneously
modify thousands, even

1233
00:58:48,740 --> 00:58:51,320
ones which might be related
to each other, like I said,

1234
00:58:51,320 --> 00:58:53,870
which is useful.

1235
00:58:53,870 --> 00:58:56,730
There's things, that CEM or
Continue Evolving from Mistakes.

1236
00:58:56,730 --> 00:58:59,210
So it actually identifies
the LLM mistakes

1237
00:58:59,210 --> 00:59:01,790
somewhat similar to what
self-improvement Chelsea was

1238
00:59:01,790 --> 00:59:04,070
talking about but
incrementally updates

1239
00:59:04,070 --> 00:59:05,396
the model to self-improve.

1240
00:59:05,396 --> 00:59:08,720

1241
00:59:08,720 --> 00:59:11,840
There's things like
lifelong mixture of experts.

1242
00:59:11,840 --> 00:59:15,110
So what it does is instead of
having a simple, fixed mixture

1243
00:59:15,110 --> 00:59:18,350
of experts architecture, it
continually adds new experts

1244
00:59:18,350 --> 00:59:23,053
for different domains over time
while freezing potentially past

1245
00:59:23,053 --> 00:59:24,470
experts which are
no longer useful

1246
00:59:24,470 --> 00:59:26,600
or don't need to be
updated to avoid things

1247
00:59:26,600 --> 00:59:28,110
like catastrophic forgetting.

1248
00:59:28,110 --> 00:59:30,660
So this is a very
smart approach.

1249
00:59:30,660 --> 00:59:32,150
Another is called CLOB.

1250
00:59:32,150 --> 00:59:35,000
So this enables continual
task learning using only

1251
00:59:35,000 --> 00:59:37,910
prompting without updating
model weights by summarizing

1252
00:59:37,910 --> 00:59:40,860
past knowledge into a
compressed prompt memory.

1253
00:59:40,860 --> 00:59:44,180
However, not a criticism of
this work, but again, this

1254
00:59:44,180 --> 00:59:47,720
is not technically
updating the brain

1255
00:59:47,720 --> 00:59:49,620
or the fundamental
capabilities of the model,

1256
00:59:49,620 --> 00:59:53,238
so this is more of a
prompt-only approach.

1257
00:59:53,238 --> 00:59:55,530
And another one of these is
called progressive prompts,

1258
00:59:55,530 --> 00:59:58,640
which, again, alerts a soft
prompt vector for each task

1259
00:59:58,640 --> 01:00:01,700
and, again, progressively
compresses them

1260
01:00:01,700 --> 01:00:03,530
and composes them together.

1261
01:00:03,530 --> 01:00:06,140
So allowing LLMs to
continually learn

1262
01:00:06,140 --> 01:00:09,860
without weight updates or
catastrophic forgetting.

1263
01:00:09,860 --> 01:00:12,500
But again, my opinion is
true continual learning would

1264
01:00:12,500 --> 01:00:16,310
update the brain or the weights
of the model in some way,

1265
01:00:16,310 --> 01:00:16,825
I guess.

1266
01:00:16,825 --> 01:00:19,370

1267
01:00:19,370 --> 01:00:20,790
So thanks.

1268
01:00:20,790 --> 01:00:22,200
That's mainly our lecture.

1269
01:00:22,200 --> 01:00:25,383
So we gave a brief overview of
transformers, how they work.

1270
01:00:25,383 --> 01:00:27,050
Talked about pre-training
and especially

1271
01:00:27,050 --> 01:00:28,890
how data is important for that.

1272
01:00:28,890 --> 01:00:32,220
Various post-training
techniques, feedback mechanisms,

1273
01:00:32,220 --> 01:00:34,210
prompting mechanisms
like chain-of-thought,

1274
01:00:34,210 --> 01:00:37,000
self-improvement, some
applications to neuroscience,

1275
01:00:37,000 --> 01:00:41,100
vision, and so forth, and some
remaining weaknesses like things

1276
01:00:41,100 --> 01:00:44,980
like the lack of continual
learning and data efficiency,

1277
01:00:44,980 --> 01:00:47,850
being able to scale down and
run these models on our phone.

1278
01:00:47,850 --> 01:00:49,480
So before we send you guys off--

1279
01:00:49,480 --> 01:00:51,840
I know we ended a bit early--

1280
01:00:51,840 --> 01:00:54,250
so this class going
forwards, every week,

1281
